{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/support.moment_saturation","record":{"id":"support.moment_saturation","kind":"claim","label":"Full contact gives both moments for every associated diffusion","status":"unverified","source_id":"support_tex_variation","locator":"variation.tex:243–246; Main.lean:44–59; Diffusion/Uniqueness.lean:13–63","statement":"For an admissible minimizer gamma, full relative support makes q(t)=t for all 0<t<1 by contact, with q(0)=0 from symmetry. Differentiating using q'(t)=E[Phi_xx(t,X_t)^2] gives E[Phi_xx(t,X_t)^2]=1 for 0<=t<1, including the right derivative at 0. Pathwise uniqueness transfers both identities from the constructed diffusion to every associated diffusion on the same Brownian system.","assumption_ids":["scalar.paper_minimizer"],"evidence_ids":["support_source_bindings_20261024","support_graph_semantic_review_pending"],"citation_ids":["support_closure_source","support_diffusion_source","support_uniqueness_lean_source","full_support_implementation"],"boundary":"Curated source-level mathematical statement, not an elaborated Lean extraction or independently accepted proof. Its displayed local hypotheses are quantified guards; a hypothetical plateau is not an added premise of the full-support theorem. The universal associated-diffusion quantifier is retained; replacing it with an existential witness would weaken the selected target. The identities are below terminal time 1, not a classical derivative assertion at 1. The minimizer premise here belongs to the ordinary manuscript model; formal-interface hypotheses remain on the separate Lean target. This classification does not prove a transport between formal models."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/support.moment_saturation","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/69473417dc487cafa5e2e39b3168d2c4a8bffdad2e91b6e486de48f122dda071","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaps","anchor":"gaps","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"short","passage_ref":"sha256:7b07c474e9e4b493474081a793473deb1d5ecae2fdcc29bf9c00dc4caa8bbe8a/papers/short.md/sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/passages/main-result","anchor":"main-result","mapping_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaps","anchor":"gaps","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n**Theorem 5.1 (full support).** In the pure zero-field SK model with\n$\\xi(t)=t^2/2$, let $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$ minimize $\\mathcal P$. Then\n$\\operatorname{supp}\\mu_\\gamma=[0,1)$ and $\\gamma$ is strictly increasing.\nFor every associated diffusion started at zero,\n$\\mathbb E u(t,X_t)^2=t$ and $\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$ for\n$0\\le t<1$.\n\nKeep the same minimiser, diffusion and moment function $q$ throughout.\nSection 3 has established $G\\ge0$, $G=0$ on the support\n$S=\\operatorname{supp}\\mu_\\gamma$, and the contact conditions\n$q(s)=s$, $q'(s)\\le1$ at support points. At zero, the value identity\ncomes from symmetry, and the slope bound requires $0\\in S$.\nSection 4 supplies curvature on constant intervals for every admissible\ncoefficient. These premises are available before any support gap is\nexcluded. We first deal with the endpoint at one, then use the curvature\nestimate on the remaining intervals.\n\nFor a terminal interval $(a,1)$, including empty support $[0,1)$,\nthe cusp estimate gives $1-q(t)\\gtrsim\\sqrt{1-t}$ and hence $G<0$\nnear one. On a positive plateau with two contact endpoints, strict\nconvexity puts $q'$ below its endpoint chord, although its integral\nmust equal the interval length. On a zero plateau the heat argument\nmakes $q'$ strictly increasing, below the right contact slope.\nAt an initial gap, use the one-sided contact slope if $0\\in S$;\notherwise the coefficient is zero and symmetry supplies $q(0)=0$.\n\n<a id=\"terminal-gap\"></a>\n\n**Why a terminal plateau violates the variational inequality.** Suppose that $\\gamma=c$ on $(a,1)$, where $0\\le a<1$ and\n$c\\ge0$ is finite. The endpoint $1$ is outside the support domain, so\nthere is no contact condition there to complete an endpoint-chord\nargument. Instead we will prove\n$$\n1-q(t)\\ge C\\sqrt{1-t}\n\\tag{5.1}\n$$\nfor some $C>0$ and every $t$ sufficiently close to one. The proof\nseparates two facts: the diffusion puts positive probability density\nnear zero, and the smoothed terminal slope has a definite deficit from\n$\\pm1$ in a shrinking window around zero.\n\nFix $s\\in(a,1)$ and let $\\rho_s$ be the law of $X_s$. Choose\n$R<\\infty$ such that $p_0=\\rho_s([-R,R])>0$. For $c>0$, set\n$$\nh(t,y)=\\mathbb E e^{c|y+\\sqrt{1-t}Z|},\\qquad\n\\Phi(t,y)=c^{-1}\\log h(t,y),\n$$\nwhere $Z$ is standard normal. The drift on this interval is\n$c\\Phi_x=h_x/h$, and the transition density is\n$$\nk_{s,t}(x,y)=p_{t-s}(y-x)\\frac{h(t,y)}{h(s,x)},\\qquad\np_v(z)=(2\\pi v)^{-1/2}e^{-z^2/(2v)}.\n\\tag{5.2}\n$$\nThe heat-semigroup identity normalises this kernel; equivalently, for\nBrownian motion started at $(s,x)$, $h(t,B_t)/h(s,x)$ is the positive\nmean-one change of measure which adds the drift $h_x/h$. For $c=0$,\nthe same kernel formula applies\nwith $h=1$, and the value function is the ordinary heat evolution of\n$|x|$. [OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3, proof of (6.6)][fs-var].\n\nPut $L=1-s$ and $\\delta=L/2$. For\n$s+\\delta\\le t<1$, $|x|\\le R$ and $|y|\\le1$,\n$$\np_{t-s}(y-x)\\ge\n(2\\pi L)^{-1/2}e^{-(R+1)^2/(2\\delta)}=:k_*>0,\n$$\nwhile $h(t,y)\\ge1$ and\n$h(s,x)\\le e^{cR}\\mathbb E e^{c\\sqrt L|Z|}=:M<\\infty$.\nIntegrating (5.2) over the part of $\\rho_s$ in $[-R,R]$ therefore\ngives a density $f_t$ of $X_t$ with\n$$\nf_t(y)\\ge p_0k_*/M=:m>0\n\\quad(s+\\delta\\le t<1,\\ |y|\\le1).\n\\tag{5.3}\n$$\nOnly positive mass of a compact set was needed at time $s$; no density\nassumption on $\\rho_s$ has entered.\n\nNow write $\\varepsilon=1-t$ and restrict to\n$|y|\\le\\sqrt\\varepsilon$. Differentiating the terminal heat formula\ngives, with the unweighted interpretation for $c=0$,\n$$\nu(t,y)=\n\\frac{\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{sgn}(y+\\sqrt\\varepsilon Z)\n                     e^{c|y+\\sqrt\\varepsilon Z|}]}\n     {\\mathbb E e^{c|y+\\sqrt\\varepsilon Z|}}.\n$$\nThe denominator is at most\n$D=e^c\\mathbb E e^{c|Z|}<\\infty$. Each sign has unnormalised\nmass at least $p_*:=\\mathbb P(Z\\ge1)>0$: the threshold\n$|y/\\sqrt\\varepsilon|$ is at most one, and the exponential weight\nis at least one. Hence each sign has tilted probability at least\n$\\theta=p_*/D\\in(0,1/2)$. It follows that\n$$\n|u(t,y)|\\le1-2\\theta,\\qquad\n1-u(t,y)^2\\ge4\\theta(1-\\theta)=:\\kappa>0.\n$$\nMultiplying this uniform deficit by the density lower bound (5.3)\nand the window width $2\\sqrt\\varepsilon$ yields\n$$\n1-q(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1-u(t,y)^2)f_t(y)\\,dy\n\\ge 2m\\kappa\\sqrt{1-t}.\n$$\nThis proves (5.1) with $C=2m\\kappa$.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3, density and terminal-window estimates][fs-var].\n\nThe sign of the contradiction is now explicit. For $s'$ sufficiently\nclose to one, the estimate holds at every $t\\in[s',1)$, so\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nG(s')&=\\int_{s'}^1(q(t)-t)\\,dt\\\\\n&\\le\\frac{(1-s')^2}{2}\n       -\\frac{2C}{3}(1-s')^{3/2}<0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe final inequality holds once $\\sqrt{1-s'}<4C/3$. It contradicts\nthe nonnegative cost of adding an atom at $s'$. This proof covers\n$c=0$ as well as $c>0$. In particular, empty support is impossible:\nthen $\\mu_\\gamma=0$ and $\\gamma=0$ on all of $[0,1)$.\nThe terminal-plateau conclusion was already recorded in\n[Auffinger, Chen and Zeng (2020), Remark 7][acz]; the estimates above\nsupply the quantitative argument needed here.\n\n<a id=\"qualitative-terminal-gap\"></a>\n\n**A shorter qualitative exclusion.** The existing curvature input also\nexcludes a terminal support gap without estimating its boundary layer.\nHere is the elementary calculus step. Suppose\n$q\\in C^1([r,1))$, $q(r)=r$, $q'(r)\\le1$, $q\\le1$, and $q'$\nis strictly convex on $(r,1)$. If $q(t)\\ge t$ at some $r<t<1$,\nstrict integration below the finite endpoint chord gives\n$$\nt-r\\le\\int_r^t q'(v)\\,dv\n <\\frac{t-r}{2}\\bigl(q'(r)+q'(t)\\bigr),\n$$\nso $q'(t)>1$. For $t<s<1$, convexity then gives\n$$\nq'(s)\\ge q'(t)+\\frac{s-t}{t-r}\\bigl(q'(t)-q'(r)\\bigr)\n\\ge q'(t)>1.\n$$\nConsequently $q(s)\\ge t+(s-t)q'(t)>1$ for $s$ sufficiently\nclose to one, a contradiction. Thus $q(t)<t$ throughout $(r,1)$.\nAll chord endpoints lie below one; no derivative at one is used.\n\nTo apply this argument independently of the quantitative proof, first\nexclude empty support by the [zero-coefficient descent](#zero-coefficient-example):\n$D_+\\mathcal P(0)[1]=1/4-1/\\pi<0$. A nonempty support with a\nterminal complementary component has a last point $r<1$. Its plateau\nhas positive height, since a neighbourhood of $r$ has positive measure.\nThe contact values and slope at $r$, including the one-sided case $r=0$,\nand positive-plateau curvature meet the calculus hypotheses. Since\n$0\\le q\\le1$, integration gives $G(r)<0$, contradicting atom addition.\nThis is an ordinary consequence of the previously stated inputs. The\nquantitative proof above is retained for its stronger estimate (5.1).\n\n**The remaining gaps have a finite right contact point.**\n\nThe support is relatively closed in $[0,1)$. Its complement is a union\nof relative open intervals, and the Stieltjes measure vanishes on each\nsuch interval. For $x<y$ in one interval,\n$$\n\\gamma(y)-\\gamma(x)=\\mu_\\gamma((x,y])=0,\n$$\nso $\\gamma$ is constant there. The terminal argument excludes a component\nending at one. Every remaining component therefore ends at a point\n$b<1$ of $S$.\n\n<a id=\"bounded-gaps\"></a>\n\nFirst consider a gap $(a,b)$ with both endpoints in $S$, allowing\n$a=0$. Compact-strip regularity gives continuity of $q'$ on $[a,b]$,\neven if $\\gamma$ jumps at an endpoint. Contact gives\n$$\nq(a)=a,\\qquad q(b)=b,\\qquad q'(a),q'(b)\\le1.\n\\tag{5.4}\n$$\nIf the constant coefficient is $c>0$, Section 4 makes $q'$ strictly\nconvex in the gap. Continuity extends the chord comparison to its\nendpoints, giving\n$$\nq'(t)<\\frac{b-t}{b-a}q'(a)+\\frac{t-a}{b-a}q'(b)\\le1,\n\\qquad a<t<b.\n\\tag{5.5}\n$$\nIntegrating contradicts $q(b)-q(a)=b-a$. Equivalently, the mean value\ntheorem applied to (5.4) requires an interior point with $q'=1$,\nwhereas (5.5) excludes every such point. The endpoint derivatives need\nonly be at most one; equality has not been assumed. With $h=q'$ and\n$C_{a,b}$ its endpoint chord, the contradiction is the exact area identity\n$$\n(b-a)-\\int_a^b h\n=(b-a)\\left(1-\\frac{h(a)+h(b)}2\\right)\n +\\int_a^b(C_{a,b}-h)>0.\n$$\nThe first term is nonnegative; strict convexity makes the second positive.\nOnly continuity of $q'$ is needed at the ends. For example, a jump\n$\\Delta\\gamma$ at $s$ gives the one-sided identity\n$$\nq''(s+)-q''(s-)=-2\\Delta\\gamma\\,\\mathbb E a(s,X_s)^3.\n$$\nIt follows by applying Itô's formula to $a^2$ and using continuity of\nits derivative moments on a compact strip. Taking $\\gamma=0$ before\n$s=1/2$ and $\\gamma=1/2$ afterward makes this jump strictly negative;\nthe terminal Cole–Hopf solution has positive Hessian. Thus continuity\nof $q'$ does not license classical higher time derivatives across\ncoefficient jumps. [OpenAI (2026a), proof of Corollary 6.4][fs-density].\n\nIf $c=0$, the separate heat argument in Section 4 makes $q'$ strictly\nincreasing. Then $q'(t)<q'(b)\\le1$ for $a<t<b$, and the same\ncontradiction follows. The left slope bound is unnecessary in this case.\n[OpenAI (2026a), proof of Theorem 1.1 after Lemma 6.3][fs-var].\n\n<a id=\"initial-gaps\"></a>\n\nThere are two distinct possibilities at the left endpoint. If\n$(0,b)$ is a gap and $0\\in S$, the preceding proof applies using\nthe one-sided contact slope bound at zero. Because $(0,b)$ contains\nno mass, the support condition at zero forces an atom there and hence\n$c=\\gamma(0)>0$. Its absence has not been proved at this stage.\nIf instead the complementary component is $[0,b)$ with $0\\notin S$,\nthere is no mass anywhere in $[0,b)$. Thus $\\gamma=0$ there.\nThe identity $q(0)=0$ comes from zero-field symmetry, and the\nright endpoint gives $q(b)=b$, $q'(b)\\le1$. Here $q''(t)>0$ for\n$0<t<b$; at zero evenness gives $\\Phi_{xxx}(0,0)=0$ and hence\n$q''(0)=0$. Strict increase on the open interval still forces $q'<1$\nthroughout $(0,b)$, an impossibility.\n\n**Full support and the moment identities.**\n\nThe cases are exhaustive, so $S=[0,1)$. Only now can contact be\napplied at every $0<t<1$, giving $q(t)=t$ throughout the domain;\nthe value at zero was already known. Differentiating and using the\ngradient-martingale identity gives\n$$\n\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=q'(t)=1,\\qquad 0\\le t<1,\n$$\nwith the right derivative at zero. Finally, every nonempty open\nsubinterval $(a,b)$ receives positive mass. Therefore\n$$\n\\gamma(b)-\\gamma(a)=\\mu_\\gamma((a,b])>0\n\\qquad(0\\le a<b<1).\n$$\nThe proof establishes full support, strict increase and the two\nmoment identities. Smooth absolute continuity, including the later\nconclusion $\\gamma(0)=0$, uses the source's further argument from\nsaturated consistency; it has not been assumed to obtain contact\nor to exclude an initial gap. [OpenAI (2026a), conclusion of the\nproof of Theorem 1.1][fs-var].\nA weaker analytic condition suffices for the bounded-gap contradiction.\nIf $h\\in C([a,b])\\cap C^2((a,b))$ has the displayed endpoint and\nintegral data, then $h'=0\\Rightarrow h''>0$ is already impossible:\neither $h\\le1$ everywhere, forcing $h\\equiv1$ by its integral, or it\nattains an interior maximum above one, where $h'=0$ and $h''\\le0$.\nThis is an elementary deduction from these premises. Chen's conditional\ncrossing theorem, with its own hypotheses, is of this form; §4 proves\nthe stronger $q'''>0$ throughout a positive plateau, without a minimizer\npremise. See [Chen, arXiv:2607.18032v1, Proposition 4.2 and the proof\nof Proposition 4.3][chen26]. Neither statement supplies terminal contact\nat the excluded endpoint one.\n","span":{"start_byte":66089,"end_byte":77343,"start_line":1460,"end_line":1720},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaps","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaps"},{"stage":"short","passage_ref":"sha256:7b07c474e9e4b493474081a793473deb1d5ecae2fdcc29bf9c00dc4caa8bbe8a/papers/short.md/sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/passages/main-result","anchor":"main-result","mapping_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n[Auffinger, Chen and Zeng (2020), Theorem 1][ACZ] proved infinite\nsupport. This need not be full support: the measure\n$\\sum_{k\\geq1}2^{-k}\\delta_{1-2^{-k}}$ has infinitely many atoms\nand many gaps. [H.-B. Chen (2026), Theorem 1.3][Chen] subsequently proved\nfull support and smooth nonnegative density. We follow OpenAI's\nquantitative proof of the support conclusion and its further\nstrict-positivity result. The feature used twice is a lower bound\nvalid throughout every positive constant interval of an admissible\ncoefficient, without a minimising assumption.\n\n**Theorem 1 (full support; [OpenAI (2026a), Theorem 1.1][FS]).**\nIf $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$ minimises $\\mathcal P$, then\n$$\n\\operatorname{supp}\\mu_\\gamma=[0,1),\\qquad\n\\gamma(t_2)>\\gamma(t_1)\\quad(0\\leq t_1<t_2<1).\n$$\nFor the solution of the displayed SDE and every $0\\leq t<1$,\n$$\nq(t)=t,\\qquad \\mathbb E[a(t,X_t)^2]=1.\n$$\n\nCorollary 4 in §4 gives the smooth density and its strict positivity\nat every interior point.\n\nExistence of a minimiser is a separate input from\n[Auffinger and Chen (2017), Theorem 1 and the proof of Lemma 3][AC].\nIntegrability allows $\\gamma$ to grow near one. Zero field gives\n$u(0,0)=0$, and $\\xi''=1$ is used in the curvature calculation;\nthe theorem here does not extend these hypotheses to nonzero field\nor a general mixed-spin covariance.\n\nOur purpose is to isolate the spatial moment common to the support and\ndensity arguments, explain why it needs an integrated estimate, and\nidentify what survives the local-replacement limit. The conclusions\nand the quantitative method are those of the cited sources.\nThe proof compares two demands on a hypothetical gap. Minimality\nforces $q$ to agree with time at its endpoints, with endpoint slopes\nat most one. On a positive constant interval a quantitative estimate\nmakes $q'$ strictly convex, so its integral is smaller than the\ninterval's length. This contradicts the required change in $q$.\nAfter excluding all gaps, the identity $q'=1$ supplies the equation\nfrom which the same estimate will force $\\gamma'>0$. This second use\nneeds the estimate for a local constant replacement that need not\nminimise, without requiring $q''=0$ at the comparison time. Its explicit\npositive margin must also survive the replacement's limit.\n\nSection 5 derives the scalar value formula and identifies the finite\nGaussian observable used by the separate QAOA theorem. These optimisation\nconsequences use full support; strict density positivity is a further\nanalytic conclusion. We assume Itô calculus and martingale convergence. Omitted\nproofs are linked to *From Parisi full support to deterministic QAOA\ncircuits: A long reasoning record*, the <span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\">[companion edition of 26 October 2026](../long/reasoning-record.md)</span>; original-source citations remain beside\nthe imported inputs.\n","span":{"start_byte":5110,"end_byte":7992,"start_line":107,"end_line":160},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/short?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=short&passage=main-result","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=short&passage=main-result"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=support.moment_saturation&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/g